 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Dynamic Frame Rate Adaptation](8221-dynamic-frame-rate-adaptation.md)
-Extends this method by tracking performance metrics to adjust resolution levels on the fly to maintain a consistently high update rate above a set threshold.
+Dynamic Frame Rate Adaptation is a runtime adjustment system that tracks rendering performance and adjusts the distribution of resolution levels on the fly to maintain a consistently high update rate above a set threshold. It solves the problem of performance instability in volume rendering, where small parametric changes (e.g., transfer function modifications) can cause frame rate dips leading to cybersickness. It sets itself apart from static resolution schemes by continuously shifting the "anchor points" between resolution levels based on the gradient of recent frame times, rather than using fixed blending factors.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(1)$ per frame for the adaptation logic. The rendering cost scales with the square of the reduction factor (assuming 2D pixel count).
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(1)$ for the adaptation state. The rendered image memory scales with the square of the reduction factor.
-*   **Strengths:** Maintains consistent frame rates under varying load; simple to implement.
-*   **Limitations:** Visual quality fluctuates; may cause noticeable popping if adaptation is too aggressive. Not given in the sources: specific performance numbers on hardware.
-*   **When to use:** Real-time applications where frame rate stability is more critical than consistent resolution.
-*   **When not to use:** Applications requiring fixed resolution for consistency (e.g., medical imaging analysis where pixel size matters).
